## Edge-set hypotheses

### $\chi^2$ Likelihood Ratio Test

The likelihood ratio test ($\chi^2$-LRT) is one of the classical ways of hypothesis testing for GGMs [@hojsgaard2012], and is implemented for SEM-CNA in the `psychonetrics` package [@epskamp2025].
It assumes that data follow a multivariate normal distribution with $\symbf{X}_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \symbf{\Sigma})$.
We can test the goodness of fit by computing the likelihood ratio (LR) of the constrained model $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp} | \mathbf{A}_\text{theo}$, and the full (saturated) model $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp} | \mathbf{A}_\text{sat}$:

\begin{equation}
T_\text{LR} = -2(\ell_{\text{restricted}} - \ell_{\text{full}}) = \chi^2_{\text{restricted}} - \chi^2_{\text{full}}
\end{equation}

where $\ell_{\text{restricted}}$ is the log-likelihood of the $\mathbf{A}_\text{theo}$-constrained model, and $\ell_{\text{full}}$ is the log-likelihood of the saturated model.
Under the $H_0$ that the constrained model is correct, $T_\text{LR}$ is $\chi^2$-distributed with $df_{\text{LR}} = df_{\text{restricted}} - df_{\text{full}}$.

### SEM fit indices 

Because the $\chi^2$-LRT often rejects conceptually adequate models [@bentler1980], various fit indices were developed to complement the decision procedure.
Here, we focus on fit indices that are commonly used in SEM and have recently been proposed for SEM-CNA [@du2025].

The root mean square error of approximation [RMSEA\; @steiger1990] belongs to the class of approximate fit indices, which compare the given model with a "perfect" saturated model, with smaller values indicating better model fit.
It quantifies the discrepancy between $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp} | \mathbf{A}_\text{theo}$ and $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp} | \mathbf{A}_\text{sat}$ in terms of the fit-function, often expressed via $\chi^2$:

\begin{equation}
\text{RMSEA} = \sqrt{\frac{\max(\chi^2 - df, 0)}{df \times (n-1)}}
\end{equation}

