Incremental fit indices compare the given model with a baseline model, which assumes full statistical independence between all variables. Here, larger values indicate better fit.
Among the indices selected for this study are the normed fit index [NFI, Type I\; @bentler1980], the Tucker-Lewis index [TLI, Type II\; @tucker1973], and the comparative fit index [CFI, Type III\; @bentler1990].

\begin{equation}
\text{NFI} = \frac{\chi^2_B - \chi^2}{\chi^2_B}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\text{TLI} = \frac{\chi^2_B/df_B - \chi^2/df}{\chi^2_B/df_B - 1}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\text{CFI} = \frac{\max(\chi^2_B - df_B, 0) - \max(\chi^2 - df, 0)}{\max(\chi^2_B - df_B, 0)}
\end{equation}

@du2025 evaluated the performance of SEM indices and their conventional cutoff values in misfit detection for the longitudinal panel vector autoregressive (*panelgvar*) model.
Most fit indices performed well, except that Type I incremental fit indices showed high false rejection rates and poorer robustness against $N$ and $p$ compared to RMSEA and Type II/III indices.
Conventional SEM cutoffs were largely generalizable in lieu of dynamic cutoffs, but stricter cutoff values for hypothesis testing or replication studies were recommended.
Moreover, their behavior is not invariant across network and sample size: All indices required a sufficiently large $N$, and some were sensitive to $p$, with performance degrading as dimensionality $p \times p$ increased.

