## Edge-weight hypotheses

### Standardized root mean square residual (SRMR)

The SRMR [@bentler2006] is commonly used as an approximate SEM fit index. 
Unlike RMSEA or incremental fit indices, it operates directly on element-wise residuals between the empirical and theoretical correlation matrices.
Therefore, we group it with other edge-weight methods and utilize it as a residual-based measure of matrix discrepancy:

\begin{equation}
\text{SRMR} = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i<j}(\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_{\text{emp},ij} - \symbf{\Sigma}_{\text{theo},ij})^2}{p(p-1)/2}},
\end{equation}

### Covariance tests for high-dimensional settings

In high-dimensional settings, where the dimension $p$ can be comparable to or larger than $N$, conventional tests like the LRT perform poorly or cannot be computed at all [e.g., @cai2013].
Even though $p > N$ is rare in psychological research, a $p$- and $N$-consistent test may be beneficial for overcoming challenges posed by $N$-sensitive indices.
Out of many available tests of the matrix-equality null hypothesis, $H_0: \Sigma = \Sigma_0$ [see review by @cai2017], we selected two that demonstrated favorable characteristics and for which implementations are readily available.

