#### Ledoit-Wolf test

@ledoit2002 proposed a test for $H_0: \Sigma = I$.
This generalizes to any null hypothesis of the form $H_0: \Sigma = \Sigma_0$ by applying a whitening transformation with respect to $\symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}$.
Whitening can be applied either to the raw data ($X^* = \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}^{-1/2}X$) or directly to the estimated covariance matrix:

\begin{equation}
\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}^* = \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}^{-1/2} \hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp} \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}^{-1/2},
\end{equation}

such that $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}^*$ is expected to equal $I$ when $H_0$ is true.
$\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}^*$ can then be plugged into the test statistic:

\begin{equation}
T_\text{LW} = \frac{1}{p}\text{tr}[(\symbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}^{*} - I)^2] - \frac{p}{n}\left(\frac{1}{p}\text{tr}(\symbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}^{*})\right)^2 + \frac{p}{n}
\end{equation}

<!-- Since regularization-based estimation methods may behave differently depending on whether the underlying matrix is more dense (if unwhitened), or sparse (which would inevitably be the case if we whitened the data with respect to $\Sigma_\text{theo}$ and $H_0$ was true), we will first estimate the network model on the original scale, then subsequently apply the whitening transformation to the resulting $\hat\Sigma_\text{emp}$. -->

This matrix-based formulation allows us to apply edge selection procedures on the original data scale before whitening the resulting $\hat{\symbf{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}$ and passing it to $T_\text{LW}$.

#### Cai-Ma test {#cai}

@cai2013 proposed a test that achieves superior power to corrected high-dimensional LRT versions, and that, like the Ledoit-Wolf test, can be applied to test $H_0: \symbf{\Sigma} = \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}$ via whitening.
However, because it employs a U-statistic over pairs of observations, only data-level ($\symbf{X}^* = \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{theo}^{-1/2}\symbf{X}$) is applicable here.
Deviations from $H_0$ are quantified by the Frobenius norm of the difference matrix $\|\symbf{\Sigma} - I\|_F^2 = \text{tr}[(\symbf{\Sigma} - I)^2]$, on which the test statistic $T_{CM}$ is based:

\begin{equation}
T_\text{CM} = \frac{2}{n(n-1)} \sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq n} h(\symbf{X}_i^*, \symbf{X}_j^*),
\end{equation}

where $h$ is calculated from any two whitened observations $\mathbf{X}_i^*$ and $\mathbf{X}_j^*$:

\begin{equation}
h(\symbf{X}_i^*, \symbf{X}_j^*) = (\symbf{X}_i^{*\prime}\symbf{X}_j^*)^2 - (\symbf{X}_i^{*\prime}\symbf{X}_i^* + \symbf{X}_j^{*\prime}\symbf{X}_j^*) + p.
\end{equation}

Under $H_0$ and assuming normally distributed observations, $T_\text{CM}$ is asymptotically normal.
We reject $H_0$ if $T_\text{CM} > c_\alpha = 2z_{1-\alpha}\sqrt{\frac{p(p+1)}{n(n-1)}}$.

### Fisher's one-sample $z$ test

Fisher's $z$ test is a traditional frequentist method introduced by @fisher1915.
Assuming approximate normality, the $z$-scores of the transformed differences between entries of $\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}$ and $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$ are tested against the null hypothesis of being zero:

\begin{equation}
z_{ij} = \frac{z(\symbf{\hat\Omega}_{\text{emp},ij}) - z(\symbf{\Omega}_{\text{theo},ij})} {\sqrt{1 / (n-p-1)}}
\end{equation}

An edge-wise multiple testing correction is applied, and the matrix-equality null is rejected if at least one edge difference is non-zero.
The test can be applied directly to $\symbf\Omega$ to ease interpretation of edge differences.
For the two-sample case, @haslbeck2022 showed that due to the multiple testing correction, the test can be rather conservative.

### Mantel Test

The Mantel test [@mantel1967] is a classical, non-parametric method for testing the correlation between two distance matrices, which does not rely on distributional assumptions.
Generally, it allows us to test $H_0: r_{g}(\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}, \symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}) = 0$ by repeatedly permuting the rows and columns of $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$ and computing its graph correlation $r_{g}$ with $\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}$, given by

\begin{equation}
r_{g} = \frac{\sum \Big( \Omega_{\text{theo},ij} - \bar{\Omega}_{\text{theo}} \Big) \Big( \hat{\Omega}_{\text{emp},ij} - \bar{\hat{\Omega}}_{\text{emp}} \Big)}{\sqrt{\sum \Big( \Omega_{\text{theo},ij} - \bar{\Omega}_{\text{theo}} \Big)^2 \sum \Big( \hat{\Omega}_{\text{emp},ij} - \bar{\hat{\Omega}}_{\text{emp}} \Big)^2}},
\end{equation}

where $\bar{\Omega}$ denotes the means of the off-diagonal entries of $\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}$ and $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$, respectively.
Like other correlation measures, $r_{g}$ ranges from $-1$ (perfectly negatively correlated) to 1 (perfectly positively correlated).
The null hypothesis of zero correlation is contrary to our intended hypothesis of matrix similarity.
It is possible, however, to reverse the direction of the hypothesis by simulating the distribution of $r_{g}$ under the $H_0$ that $\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}$ and $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$ are identical, and to subsequently test whether the observed $|r_{g}|$ is significantly smaller than $1$ (i.e., $H_0: r_g(\symbf{\Omega}_\text{theo}, \symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}) = 1$) instead.

## Estimation methods for model selection {#estimation-methods}

In applied network analysis, researchers may not typically work with the fully saturated network estimated from the data, but employ estimation methods to remove small or spurious edges from consideration [@epskamp2018]. 
However, despite sometimes being treated this way, estimation procedures do not constitute formal statistical inference and may introduce systematic bias [@williams2019; @williams2020d]; and network inference especially with regularized estimation remains an active area of research [@waldorp2024]. 
We therefore incorporate a selection of prevalent estimation techniques alongside the confirmatory methods to assess how estimation impacts subsequent test performance.

### Regularized estimators

One of the most popular estimation methods is the graphical least absolute shrinkage and selection operator [*glasso*, @friedman2008].
To determine the appropriate degree of regularization, it is often applied in combination with the extended Bayesian Information Criterion (EBIC) for model selection.
It employs a convex $\ell_1$ regularization penalty to obtain sparse, interpretable model solutions. 
However, this comes at the cost of favoring overly sparse networks and underestimating larger edges (shrinkage bias) especially in smaller samples, while retaining weak false-positive edges and thus exhibiting lower specificity in denser networks and larger samples [@isvoranu2023; @williams2019].

While regularization methods have been the subject of frequent criticism [@williams2019; @williams2020], some techniques have been shown to perform better in recovering the true population matrix than others [@waldorp2024].
One promising candidate is the nonconvex arctangent (*atan*) regularization penalty [@wang2016].
It also penalizes for sparsity, but introduces less bias for larger coefficients, allowing for better recovery of the true sparsity pattern.
Including it allows us to examine whether reduced shrinkage bias translates to improved performance in confirmatory testing.

### Nonregularized estimators

An unregularized approach is GGM estimation combined with stepwise model selection [*ggmModSelect*, @epskamp2025]. 
