Unlike glasso regularization, it re-estimates nonzero parameters without shrinkage, leading to asymptotic consistency for higher $N$.
The second is *nodewise regression* (or neighborhood selection) with Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) forward selection [@yang2015].
In this approach, all nodes are treated as predictors of one node at a time, and the predictor that minimizes the BIC is selected first.
Predictors are incrementally added until BIC can no longer be minimized.
In comparative evaluations, nodewise regression performed well at moderate to large $N$, and showed favorable trade-offs between sensitivity and specificity [@isvoranu2023].
Varying sparsity of the true networks had minimal impact on the results of nodewise regression compared to glasso [@nehler2024a; @williams2019], which may be beneficial if density differences are a feature of interest.

## Expectations

Based on the properties of the reviewed methods, when $H_0$ is true, we expect the high-dimensional covariance tests (Ledoit-Wolf, Cai-Ma) to maintain $\alpha$ error rates closer to nominal $\alpha$ than the $\chi^2$-LRT and SEM indices, especially in small samples or higher dimensions.
When $H_0$ is false -- that is, when the theoretical and empirical networks differ -- all methods should show increasing power with increasing $N$ and dissimilarity between $\symbf\Omega_\text{theo}$ and $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$, with superior power of high-dimensional covariance tests in conditions with higher $p$.
Consistently with prior findings [@du2025], we expect $\chi^2$-LRT and SEM indices to be sensitive to both $N$ and rising dimensionality $p$.
In line with @haslbeck2022, we expect Fisher's $z$-test to be more conservative, particularly for larger $p$, due to the increasing number of pairwise comparisons and the associated multiple testing correction.
Regarding estimation methods, we expect atan, ggmModSelect, and nodewise to yield less biased edge weight estimates than glasso, potentially leading to better performance of the hypothesis tests that operate on the estimated network.
We expect the regularized estimators' -- most prominently glasso's -- tendency to produce sparser networks to systematically affect performance: 
Under misspecification, we expect reduced power to detect an overly dense $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$ (as the estimation method will shrink added edges to zero, counteracting the direction of misspecification). 
Conversely, we expect to observe elevated power to detect an overly sparse $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$, as the shrinkage bias coincides with the direction of misspecification
<!-- , though only for methods sensitive to this direction of misspecification -->
.
Under $H_0$, this bias should result in elevated $\alpha$ error rates
<!-- for tests which are sensitive to detecting reduced density, while tests that are unable to do so will show nominal or lower $\alpha$ error rates due to overretaining $H_0$. -->
.

